+\clearpage\section*{S8. Fixed 4-dB development examples and preserved 10-dB view}
+The main Fig.~3 uses the same previously selected Pastries (development index 95) and Dog (index 21), at $N=1024$ and 4~dB. They are not a random or representative common500 sample. The five historical methods use their original first formal timing repetition (\texttt{r3}); their ten recovered float arrays match the original recorded hashes exactly. Only the two missing WHOLE and two missing Ada events are new. All four new receptions succeeded. No selection, policy recalibration, training, or population-level statistical test was performed.
+
+WHOLE uses the frozen target $(7,0)$ and QPSK rate 2/3, falling back to $(6,0)$ for both images. It sends 974 source bits per image within the 1245-bit source capacity. Ada reuses the original predicted classes and independently terminated source streams; its paid prefixes contain 103 and 101 tokens for Pastries and Dog. The sampled tail retains CFG=4, $T=1$, top-$k=2000$, and top-$p=1$.
+
+The original method-specific noise identities are retained: NeST/ARPC use 6201 and Latent/Swin/BPG use 2001. The four new visualization events have a separately registered namespace, with WHOLE seed 6201 and Ada seed 2001. Equal seed labels do not assert identical noise vectors across methods. Metrics below are computed from the displayed native float32 reconstructions using the frozen LPIPS-Alex and DreamSim loaders. PNGs use only clipping and rounding to uint8; there is no crop, enhancement, or repair.
+
+For these two images, NeST has lower LPIPS and DreamSim than WHOLE. Both next-scale reconstructions nevertheless change croissant shapes. Latent JSCC has lower LPIPS than NeST in both cases and lower DreamSim for Pastries. These local observations do not replace the common500 results or the 10-dB Ada advantage reported in the main text.
